    description: "FILED 2026-09-26 as WI-bulaz's step 3 (\"Disclose it. Most likely a caveat kind,\nraised ONLY WHERE IT DISCRIMINATES\"). WI-bulaz did steps 1 and 2: a partially\nparsed file is counted and carried in `limits.failed_files` with a\n`partial_parse:` reason. No verdict reads it yet.\n\nWHY A CAVEAT ON \"ANY PARTIAL PARSE\" WOULD NOT DISCRIMINATE. Surveys with the\ndisclosure wired (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/bulaz_09262026/*.wired.json and the\nearlier *.json):\n\n    repo           partial files   mostly\n    phoenix        114             elixir (112)\n    crun            74             .c 57, .h 16\n    AFNetworking    34             .h 31 (29 of them the C grammar on ObjC,\n                                   see WI-somod)\n    koel            12             javascript 11\n    hummingbird      7             swift 5\n    alertmanager     0\n\nRaw tree-sitter census (has_error per file): crun C 65 of 127; sherpa-onnx .h\n427 of 504; phoenix .ex/.exs 112 of 291. In C-family and Elixir a partial\nparse is the norm, so a caveat raised on any of them would be on nearly every\nverdict there and would carry no information.\n\nWHAT WOULD DISCRIMINATE, candidates to measure, not decided:\n  (a) Scope: raise only when a partially parsed file is in the claim's reach,\n      i.e. it holds a function on a reported path or would hold the sink/source\n      the refutation depends on. A clean verdict's refutation over a function\n      whose file parsed cleanly does not depend on the damaged file.\n  (b) Magnitude: damaged-node count per KLOC, calibrated per grammar (the\n      fortran trap: a lagging grammar flags valid code).\n  (c) Precondition: WI-somod first, so C/ObjC cross-dialect rows do not count.\n\nEnvelope change: a new caveat kind is a machine-surface change (schema version),\nper the row's step 2 note."  # fecb
    fields: {}  # fecb
