- op: discuss  # 622f
  at: '2026-09-28T01:12:33Z'  # 622f
  by: agent  # 622f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 622f
  clock: 3  # 622f
  nonce: 622f  # 622f
  message: "MEASURED; NO CAVEAT SHIPPED; RE-SCOPED. Docs: PR #1254 (VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md now states that no verdict consults limits.failed_files, with the numbers below).\n\nPrecondition (c) is done: WI-somod (#1253) removed the cross-dialect .h rows.\n\nCandidate (a), scope to the claim's reach -- does NOT discriminate. Prototype: for each built-in source label, BFS from source callers over calls, files reached vs partial_parse files (surveys ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/somod_09272026/*.B.json). crun: host_secret reach spans 108 files, 30 of the 66 partial ones; host_description 32; untrusted_input 4. sherpa-onnx: 4 / 17 / 6 of 132. Every label on both repos reaches at least one, so an \"any partial file in reach\" caveat fires on every taint verdict there.\n\nCandidate (b), magnitude -- the measures do NOT track loss. Damaged-line share (outermost ERROR/MISSING lines): crun median 2%, but signals.c / mount_flags.c (gperf output) are 100% and keep 3/3 and 4/4 functions; AFURLSessionManager.m is 100% and keeps 84/84 methods (analyzers walk into ERROR subtrees). Skipped-token share (leaf tokens directly under ERROR) has the same problem. And the loss that DOES happen is invisible to both: AFHTTPSessionManager.h and AFURLSessionManager.h emit 0 of 4 and 0 of 11 @property declarations, and no ERROR node surrounds them -- the grammar misread the @interface block and recovered the properties as top-level expression_statements.\n\nThe row's premise, corrected: \"in Elixir a partial parse is the norm\" does not hold. All 112 phoenix partial parses are EEx generator templates (.ex files full of <%= %>), not Elixir. C/C++ premise holds.\n\nWHAT WOULD UNBLOCK IT: a per-file LOSS signal rather than a damage signal -- e.g. a lexical count of declaration-shaped lines per language (func/def/@property/method heads) compared with what the analyzer emitted for the file, reported as \"N declaration(s) in this file were not extracted\". That discriminates by construction (0 on signals.c, 4 on AFHTTPSessionManager.h). It is a per-language instrument and needs its own measurement; until it exists, any caveat here is the blanket one the row rules out.\n\nFiled separately (a real defect the measurement surfaced): objc headers whose @interface block the grammar misreads lose their @property declarations."  # 622f
